From: Luke Howard <lukeh@padl.com>
Subject: [PATCH] build-script-helper: build swift-driver with the native build system

swift-driver's Utilities/build-script-helper.py drives the freshly built
`swift-build` with no --build-system flag, so it gets SwiftPM 6.4's default
swiftbuild engine. That engine deadlocks when driven from the toolchain
build: CoreFoundation fails to create its runloop wakeup socket pair, the
notification that a child process exited never arrives, and swift-build sits
in sigsuspend() indefinitely with zombie clang children while the machine
goes completely idle. Nothing times out, so the build hangs rather than
failing.

Observed at `--- Installing swiftdriver ---`, 7+ hours with no output:

    swift-build   state S   wchan sigsuspend
      \_ [clang] <defunct>

This is the same defect already worked around in swiftpm's
Utilities/bootstrap; swift-driver is a second, independent entry point into
it. SwiftPM exposes no environment variable for the engine -- --build-system
is the only selector -- so each caller has to be fixed separately.

Setting it in get_swiftpm_options() covers both swiftpm() and
swiftpm_bin_path(), which share those arguments.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [workaround for an upstream bug, not yet reported]
---
--- a/Utilities/build-script-helper.py
+++ b/Utilities/build-script-helper.py
@@ -113,6 +113,13 @@
 
 def get_swiftpm_options(args):
     swiftpm_args = [
+        # SwiftPM defaults to the swiftbuild engine from 6.4 on, which
+        # deadlocks when driven from the toolchain build: CoreFoundation
+        # cannot create its runloop wakeup socket pair, child-exit
+        # notifications never arrive, and the process sits in sigsuspend()
+        # forever with zombie clang children. Use the legacy engine.
+        "--build-system",
+        "native",
         "--package-path",
         args.package_path,
         "--scratch-path",
